WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
Confirmation of a falcon 9 stage, striking the moon, test deep space tracking networks while exposing gaps in lunar debris protocols ahead of crewed missions. This is SpaceX Daily, episode 60. SpaceX is preparing a starship flight as early as late august that would attempt the first catch of the ship upper stage by the launch tower. The mission profile also targets the first orbital deployment of starlink satellites from starship. Ground teams are completing final vehicle

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:59.960
checks at starbase while the tower arms undergo software and hydraulic validation. Success would mark a major step toward full vehicle reuse by demonstrating that the lighter upper stage can be recovered without a down-range landing site. This development advances the ongoing starship reuse goals by testing whether the upper stage can close the loop at the launch site itself. NASA verified that a falcon 9 upper stage impacted the lunar surface at approximately five minutes.

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:09.580
5,400 miles per hour, near the crater Einstein, ending an 18-month coast that began after a 2024 lunar lander mission.

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:29.560
Tracking data from multiple ground stations matched the predicted impact point within seconds. The event served as an unplanned test of cis lunar object cataloging methods. South Korea's Denuri Orbiter returned high-resolution images showing a fresh crater formed by the falcon 9 upper stage impact.

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:53.580
The before and after pairs allow precise measurement of ejected distribution and crater morphology. Analysts are using the data to refine models of hypervelocity impacts on regolith. NASA and Ross Cosmos extended their seed exchange deal through upcoming crew dragon and Soyuz rotations, ensuring continued crew transport redundancy for the ISS.

00:01:54.580 --> 00:02:08.360
The agreement does not yet include Boeing Starliner, leaving Dragon as the sole US vehicle in the barter. Both agencies cited stable technical interfaces and shared logistics needs as the basis for continuation.

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:20.280
SpaceX confirmed that construction on the Grimes County Terrafab site will begin within months after local briefings addressed resident concerns over traffic, noise, and water use.

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:29.200
The facility is sized to support parallel production of starship components and starlink satellites at rates required for daily launch cadence.

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:45.480
County officials received updated site plans showing expanded rail spurs and onsite power generation. SpaceX acquired $295 million worth of Tesla megapacks during the second quarter, more than doubling its prior quarterly spend.

00:02:45.480 --> 00:03:01.840
The units are being installed at facilities supporting AI training clusters that draw power through starlink backhaul links. Procurement records show the company is prioritizing high density rack configurations, suited for rapid deployment at remote sites.

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:20.140
This hardware directly supports the growing compute load tied to orbital data center concepts under study. During its first earnings call, SpaceX outlined direct to cell service that it says will exceed the coverage and latency of the three largest US carriers in rural and maritime zones.

00:03:20.660 --> 00:03:28.940
The system uses the existing starlink constellation plus new spectrum agreements to deliver voice and data without terrestrial towers.

00:03:28.940 --> 00:03:40.960
Gwynne Shotwell noted the combined annual revenue of the target carriers exceeds $600 billion, framing the service as a high margin add on once regulatory approvals clear.

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:57.420
Initial beta testing is expected to begin with partner devices later this year. SpaceX detailed a standalone mobile network that would use starlink satellites to provide nationwide coverage, initially targeting areas underserved by terrestrial infrastructure.

00:03:57.420 --> 00:04:19.600
The service would integrate with existing phone hardware through software updates and new spectrum licenses regulatory filings indicate the company is seeking primary carrier status rather than wholesale roaming agreements users on our slash SpaceX lounge are debating where to begin studying cryogenic propellant management for orbital refueling.

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:36.860
Noting that autogenous pressurization data remains scarce outside internal industry papers. Another thread explores placing compute satellites in lunar orbit to avoid earth-based light pollution and atmospheric reentry effects while accepting the one second latency penalty.

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:46.860
Participants calculate that delta V costs from LEO to low lunar orbit are only about 40% higher than reaching LEO itself.

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:58.860
One thing worth watching is how wayward Falcon 9 hardware reaching the lunar surface has renewed calls for binding international rules on end of life disposal for cis lunar trajectories.

00:04:59.480 --> 00:05:07.740
Without coordinated tracking standards future high traffic lunar operations risk accumulating untracked debris at key orbital nodes.

00:05:08.740 --> 00:05:20.280
On the AI front SpaceX and NVIDIA are jointly developing the star-mind AI-1 orbital supercomputer payload with AMD shares reacting to the exclusive NVIDIA commitment.

00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:31.960
A Reddit analysis proposes lunar orbit compute satellites to sidestep earth sky brightness and reentry concerns while accepting modest latency for long duration training runs.

00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:42.100
SpaceX's Q2 Megapack purchases are explicitly tied to scaling AI data center capacity that will eventually integrate with star link backhaul.

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:52.180
From an engineering standpoint catching the ship upper stage on the tower introduces a fundamentally different control problem than recovering the super heavy booster.

00:05:52.180 --> 00:05:58.140
The ship lacks the booster's eight sea level raptors and their high thrust vectoring authority.

00:05:58.660 --> 00:06:07.860
So residual propellant margins after deorbit must be allocated with tighter precision to keep touchdown velocities inside the tower arms capture envelope.

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:14.020
Autogenous pressurization during any final propellant settling burn adds another variable.

00:06:14.020 --> 00:06:23.940
Ullage gas temperatures directly affect the density of the remaining liquid shifting the vehicle's center of mass by centimeters that the catch arms must accommodate.

00:06:24.420 --> 00:06:33.140
Because the ship's dry mass is roughly one fifth that of the booster its structural stiffness under lateral loads from the tower arms is lower.

00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:41.720
Designers must therefore trade between arm compliance and the risk of inducing bending modes that could crack tiles already stressed by reentry.

00:06:41.720 --> 00:06:50.860
The first principles limit here is the available impulse from cold gas or remaining RCS thrusters once the main engines are shut down.

00:06:51.400 --> 00:07:00.660
Any excess propellant carried for margin directly reduces payload to orbit so the catch sequence must be validated with the smallest possible propellant overhead.

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:08.300
If the August attempt succeeds it collapses the recovery logistics chain from two separate landing zones to a single tower.

00:07:08.300 --> 00:07:16.900
Removing the need for downrange drone ship assets and shortening the turnaround clock by the time previously spent on booster transport.

00:07:17.240 --> 00:07:25.660
The next observable milestone will be whether post catch inspections reveal tile or skirt damage levels compatible with rapid reuse.

00:07:26.380 --> 00:07:34.960
The same metric that ultimately determines whether starship can close the cost per kilogram curve required for routine lunar and Mars traffic.

